{{/*
string_format_check emits a "format" assertion against a value already known to
be a Go string.

There is one arm, and it dispatches to the shared helper for the format. Every
position that asserts a format comes through here -- a struct field, an alias
over its own value, and the wrapper a format with no declared "type" resolves
to -- so the three cannot disagree about what a format means, which they did for
as long as each carried a copy of the checks. See format_helpers.

.Expr  a Go expression of type string
.Path  what to name the value in the error message ("value", a JSON pointer, ...)
.Value the format keyword

An unrecognised format renders nothing: `format` is an annotation unless
something can judge it, and formatCheckableOnString has already refused to build
a rule for one nothing judges.
*/}}
{{define "string_format_check"}}
{{- $v := .Expr}}
{{- $path := .Path}}
{{- $fn := formatHelperName .Value .StringBacked}}
{{- if $fn}}
if _err := {{$fn}}({{$v}}); _err != nil {
	return fmt.Errorf("{{$path}}: %w", {{if .Args}}{{.Args}}, {{end}}_err)
}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
